# Phylogenetic Analysis

## Overview

Phylogenetic analysis is used to study the evolutionary relationships among biological sequences by constructing a phylogenetic tree.
It helps determine the degree of relatedness between species or proteins based on sequence similarity. In this project, the aligned
protein sequences obtained from Multiple Sequence Alignment (MSA) were used to generate a phylogenetic tree using MEGA X, and the
resulting tree was visualized and annotated using iTOL.

## Objective

The objective of this analysis was to infer the evolutionary relationships among homologous protein sequences and visualize their
divergence and common ancestry through a phylogenetic tree.

## Methodology

The multiple sequence alignment generated by Clustal Omega was imported into MEGA X for phylogenetic tree construction using an
appropriate evolutionary method. The generated tree was saved in Newick format and further uploaded to iTOL for visualization, annotation,
and customization to improve its interpretation and presentation.

## Input

* Multiple sequence alignment file (.aln or FASTA).
* MEGA-compatible alignment (.meg, if applicable).

## Output

* Phylogenetic tree.
* Newick tree file (.nwk).
* Annotated tree visualization.
* Exported tree image (.png, .pdf, or .svg).

## Key Features

* Infers evolutionary relationships among sequences.
* Supports multiple tree-building methods.
* Provides bootstrap analysis for tree reliability.
* Produces publication-quality tree visualizations.

## Applications

* Evolutionary studies.
* Comparative genomics and proteomics.
* Species and protein relationship analysis.
* Functional and evolutionary inference.

## Files Included

* Multiple sequence alignment file.
* MEGA project/session file (.meg, if applicable).
* Newick tree file (.nwk).
* Final annotated phylogenetic tree image.

## Conclusion

Phylogenetic analysis successfully revealed the evolutionary relationships among the selected protein sequences. The constructed tree
provided a clear representation of sequence divergence and common ancestry, while visualization using iTOL enhanced the interpretation 
and presentation of the phylogenetic results.

